# integrations/claude-code/drive, as an ENTRY (design/caos-expr.md, Phase 3):
# evaluating this directory yields the image that runs drive.go as a worker.
# std/go's /worker `go run`s whatever is bound as `worker1`.
#
# THIS FILE IS WHERE CALLING IT IS DOCUMENTED. The `help` below is the tool's
# own description — the copy a harness offers a model, and the copy a person
# reads — so there is no second one to drift (the note std/merge/.caos-expr
# carries, for the same reason). drive.go's comments cover how it works;
# README.md is an overview and points here.
#
# NOTHING IS BOUND HERE. A caller curries the verb, its arguments, and the time:
#
#   caos-cli run --base:@=integrations/claude-code/drive \
#     --verb=list --at="$(date +%s)"
#   caos-cli run --base:@=integrations/claude-code/drive --verb=start \
#     --env=Caos --repo=Metta-AI/caos-session --prompt='the first prompt' \
#     --at="$(date +%s)"
#
# The prompt is a plain string argument. `:@=` would ingest a FILE, and it
# ingests only git-tracked paths — an ad-hoc prompt is not one.
#
# `at` IS REQUIRED BY EVERY VERB, and it is the current time. A worker's result
# is memoized on its ArgTree and nothing here is a pure function of its
# arguments, so without a value that moves, a second `start` answers with the
# first session's id and creates nothing, and a second `list` answers with a
# listing from whenever the first one ran. A coarser time (`date +%Y%m%d%H%M`)
# deliberately reuses an answer for up to a minute. It could not be called
# `salt`: that is a RESERVED arg name (llm-step's RESERVED_ARGS).
#
# No `@writer` and no `@in`: nothing here advances the conversation tree.
HELP=<<END
Start and inspect Claude Code cloud sessions. A session runs in a named cloud
environment, on a named GitHub repository, and answers prompts. `verb` chooses
what to do, and the other parameters belong to the verb: `start` takes env,
repo and prompt and returns a session id; `send` takes session and prompt;
`list` takes nothing and returns recent sessions with their status and
environment; `info` takes session and returns its environment, its source and
its provisioning trace, read from outside the container; `conv` takes session
and locates the caos conversation it records into; `archive` takes session and
ends it, freeing its container; `probe` asks a session to dump what it can see
of its own container, into its transcript rather than into this result, and
takes either a session or an env and repo to start a fresh one. Environments
are separately addressable: `env-list` takes nothing, `env-show` takes env, and
`env-update`, `env-create` and `env-delete` CHANGE, CREATE and DESTROY an
environment that other sessions may be using. A session id is `cse_...` or
`session_...`, two spellings of the same session, and either is accepted
wherever one is asked for. Starting a session costs a container and about two
minutes before it can answer, so read the reply back with `info` rather than
expecting one from `start`.
@param verb One of start, send, list, info, conv, archive, probe, env-list, env-show, env-update, env-create, env-delete.
@param at The current time as seconds since the epoch. The result is memoized on the arguments, so a value used before returns that earlier answer instead of asking again — pass the time now, not a constant.
@param {string} [env] Cloud environment, by name or by its env_... id.
@param {string} [repo] Repository the session opens: owner/repo, owner/repo@ref, or a full URL.
@param {string} [prompt] Prompt text, for start and send.
@param {string} [session] Session id (cse_... or session_...) or a claude.ai/code URL.
@param {string} [ref] Branch, tag or commit to check out; the repository's default when omitted.
@param {string} [title] The session's title; the prompt's first line when omitted.
@param {string} [from] env-create only: an existing environment to copy.
@param {string} [rename] env-update only: a new name for the environment.
@param {string} [init-script] env-update only: the environment's new setup script, in full.
@param {array} [set-env] env-update only: environment variables to set, each as KEY=VALUE.
@param {array} [unset-env] env-update only: names of environment variables to remove.
END
curry --base:@=DEEP-DEPS/go --worker1:@=drive.go --help=$HELP
